WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
11 pages asked the justices to keep discounted rates for party political ads. This is Omni View North America, episode 19. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. The Federal Communications Commission is asking the US Supreme Court in an 11-page filing to preserve discounted rates for certain political party ads and for joint fundraising committees. Solicitor General D. John Sauer filed over the weekend and is urging the court to block a lower court order that would require the agency to unwind guidance

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.900
on what local radio and television stations may charge for political advertising,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.720
the desk reports. The dispute turns on a March 30 public notice that extended the lowest unit

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.540
charge to qualifying political party advertisements and to certain joint fundraising committees.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.860
Chief Justice John Roberts on Thursday temporarily stayed a fourth-circuit order that had

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.260
required the FCC to decide the dispute by noon eastern time on Friday after the federal government

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.420
sought emergency relief. Four Democratic candidates, former Ohio Senator Sherrod Brown,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.640
Georgia Senator John Assoff, former North Carolina Governor Roy Cooper, and Michigan

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.700
Representative Kristen McDonald-Rivitt, argue the March guidance unlawfully widened to benefit

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.840
federal law reserves for legally qualified candidates. That statutory benefit is the lowest

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.900
available rate in the 45 days before primary and in the 60 days before a general election.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.980
The FCC says the March guidance clarifies existing requirements rather than creating

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:26.720
new rules.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.660
Opponents of the change say it could benefit Republican candidates in battleground

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.800
states ahead of the midterms, and that it would cut revenue stations need to operate.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.400
In August, the fourth circuit struck down the guidance, finding that political parties

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.760
in joint fundraising committees with non-Canada members were not entitled to the discounts.

