00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.280
would, starting next year, send all state sales tax collected on sporting goods to land

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.100
conservation, access and wildfire mitigation.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.800
The paper estimates that amount at about $175 million at the start.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.100
The diversion would shrink or turn off in any year it would affect the family affordability tax credit,

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.960
or the earned income tax credit.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.900
Those two credits totaled nearly $1 billion in 2024.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.240
The family credit accounted for $866 million of that total.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:42.620
The family credit offers households earning as much as $96,000 up to $3,200 per child under six,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.600
and up to $2,400 per child ages 6 to 16.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.260
Tarn Udall, an attorney with Western Resource Advocates,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.460
a main backer, says the measure was built to fund protection

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.600
from wildfire and drought while recognizing budget pressure.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.120
Udall also says that any money for land management is a gain.

